Falsifies `inter_process_communication::verification::removing_a_waiter_preserves_the_invariant`.

The defect: `take_out` notes the hit and then keeps the victim anyway. The drain-and-repush
shape invites it: the loop's job reads as "rebuild the queue", the `else` is the only thing that
drops anything, and the function still returns the right answer to every caller that only asks
whether the waiter was there.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert!(found.is_none() || e.is_idle())`, on a hit. The invariant
assertion survives, because the queue the tag names still holds a thread, which is why this
harness states both: a remove that reports success while leaving the thread queued is how
milestone 133 (ending a permanently blocked thread) ends a thread and then finds it again on a
wait queue.

The queues take `Unqueued` tokens, and one token cannot be both pushed and returned, so the defect
is written the only way it can be, by minting a second token with `unsafe`. That this takes an
`unsafe` block a reviewer has to sign is the token doing its job; the harness is still shown to
catch the result.

